      {
        "id": 87101,
        "name": "Ronway",
        "username": "Ronway",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ronway/{size}/1856_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-10-21T15:13:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Sefirit\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/s/e68b1a/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Sefirit:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>Ronway wrote:</b><br><blockquote>&nbsp;skoticus wrote:<br><blockquote>But what if there is a card (an environment card, most likely) that says \"A hero may skip their turn to...\" etc. Would this mean that start of turn and end of turn effects don't trigger, since there is no turn? Would scholar then be able to skip his turn to keep Flesh to Iron in play without paying the price?&nbsp;\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\nIt would depend on what Start of Turn card was in play first. As Start of Turn effects are triggered in the order of play. End of Turn effects will be completely skipped this way.\n</blockquote></div>\nIs this still true with the newer Order of Ops ruling.&nbsp;&nbsp; Or do you have more choice over the order of when things trigger similar to if Legacy has &lt;Those two cards out that I can't remember but hopefully people will get through context&gt; out and is going to be hit for 5 damage.&nbsp; It used to be he took either 2 or 4 depending on the order they came out, but now you can choose the order of resolution to make is 2.&nbsp; Maybe that rule doesn't apply to start and end of turn effects in the same way.&nbsp; I know that when I'm resolving a villain turn I follow the order that cards came out, but I wasn't sure if that was still necessary or if its just an easy way to keep track of things.&nbsp;\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Cards that trigger at the start and end of turn phases still trigger in the order that they entered play. So yes, this is how it will be played out.</p>",
